Heat-related illness
Social & Environmental
Core body temperature >40C, Altered mental status, Hot flushed skin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-032
Reason for Visit: Acute collapse and severe hyperthermia during a prolonged residential power outage in a heatwave.
Location: Private residence (top-floor apartment)
Present at Visit: Patient, neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient found confused and lethargic in an un-air-conditioned apartment during a 41-degree heatwave. Urgent assessment was required to treat impending heatstroke, initiate active cooling, and prevent irreversible neurological damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I feel so dizzy, I can't think straight, and my legs are cramping horribly."
History of Present Illness: The neighbor reports the patient was found slumped in a chair, confused, and unable to answer the door properly. Over the past 8 hours, the patient experienced a progressive onset of extreme fatigue, weakness, and debilitating headache. Key symptoms escalated to a profound alteration of mental status and delirium. The patient reports a complete cessation of sweating over the last two hours. There is a history of being trapped in a hot, humid environment due to a lack of air conditioning (power outage). The patient complains of severe, painful muscle cramps in the lower extremities and nausea without vomiting. Dark urine indicating severe dehydration was noted in the toilet.
Functional Status: Normally independent; currently incapacitated by hyperthermia and delirium
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme environmental hazard (indoor temperature >38C), social isolation, imminent risk of fatal hyperthermia

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/50 | HR: 130 | Temp: 39.8 | RR: 26 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Hot, flushed, and entirely dry skin; visibly confused, sluggish responses, panting
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals disorientation to time and place (GCS 13), with slurred speech but no focal motor deficits. Cardiovascular exam shows profound tachycardia with weak, thready radial pulses and delayed capillary refill (>4 seconds). Abdomen is soft. Diagnostic tetrad features are highly suspected/present: core body temperature approaching 40C, altered mental status, and hot flushed skin (classic non-exertional heatstroke).
Environmental Observations: Apartment is stiflingly hot, windows are closed, no fans are operating due to the power outage.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient takes regular diuretics (furosemide) which likely exacerbated the profound volume depletion.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Heat-related illness (Classic Heatstroke / Severe Dehydration) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Septic Shock - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The direct environmental correlation, hot/dry skin rather than clammy/sweaty skin, and the absence of a focal infective source.
2. Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The global nature of the confusion without unilateral weakness or facial droop, directly resolving with initial cooling measures.
3. Hypoglycemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Capillary blood glucose reading of 6.2 mmol/L, making neuroglycopenia an unlikely cause of the delirium.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in acute, life-threatening multi-system crisis due to classic non-exertional heatstroke, evidenced by a core temperature of nearly 40C, profound hypotension, and altered mentation. Deferring assessment would have inevitably resulted in cardiovascular collapse, rhabdomyolysis, and death. The objective findings of hyperthermia and delirium completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to rapidly initiate active external cooling, provide fluid resuscitation, and arrange immediate emergency transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate initiation of active cooling measures: removing excess clothing, applying cold, wet towels to the groin and axillae, and using a battery-powered fan (from clinician's vehicle). Administration of oral rehydration solution if the patient can swallow safely. Urgent dispatch of an ambulance.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously sipping the cool fluid and allowing us to keep these wet towels on you, you rapidly draw the dangerous heat out of your core organs, which will clear your confusion and protect your brain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None acutely; environmental relocation is mandatory.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department for IV cooling and continuous monitoring.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the Emergency Department. If the patient begins to seize or loses consciousness entirely prior to ambulance arrival, place them in the recovery position and maintain an open airway while continuing cooling measures.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Heatstroke requires aggressive, hospital-based intravenous cooling and hydration to prevent permanent organ damage. You cannot stay in this apartment until the power is restored.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to stand up or walk. The ambulance is coming. Keep sipping the fluid slowly so you don't choke.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The extreme heat in your apartment has overwhelmed your body's ability to sweat, causing your internal temperature to rise dangerously high. This is causing the confusion. The wet towels are acting as artificial sweat to cool you down safely.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely. Let the paramedics handle your transport. Once discharged from the hospital, you must stay with family or in an air-conditioned public shelter until your home's power is fixed.

